  {
   "id": "ZEsCX0sgDgAJ",
   "from": "Mike Lawrence",
   "author": "dae01c57a3c54a3a",
   "date": "2017-03-03T15:01:33Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] simple math q for transformed data section",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I was actually able to solve this for the special case I was considering (details in this thread: <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/forum/#!category-topic/stan-users/MAKPeWvqbFs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/forum/%23!category-topic/stan-users/MAKPeWvqbFs&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792458398389000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3r2Xm_FOFKJb6tHS2oOugD\">https://groups.google.com/forum/#!category-topic/stan-users/MAKPeWvqbFs</a> ), which for this thread boils down to doing:<div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><div>data {</div><div>  vector[N] Y ;</div><div>  matrix[N,K] X ;</div><div>}</div><div>transformed data <span style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif\">{</span><br></div><div>  vector[N] obs ;</div><div>  obs = inverse(transpose(X) * X) * transpose(X) * Y ;</div><div>}</div><div><br></div></div></code></div><br><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
